{#- One edition of the weekly.

    The Hebrew is given whole. It is ours, it is the reason the page is worth indexing,
    and a stranger who can read it is the reader this exists for. What stays behind the
    door is the product: the English beside every sentence, and the word cards. -#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
{% include '_public_head.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('public.css') }}{{ asset('front.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="public front">
{#- The front door's bar (2026-09-27). -#}
{% with home = '/', here = '/weekly/' ~ issue.id ~ '/' ~ level.value %}{% include '_front_bar.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
<main class="sheet weekly">
  {#- A landing page for one thing: reading the news in Hebrew, drawn as the front door is
      (2026-09-27). The pitch is in English because the person it is for cannot yet read
      the Hebrew — that is the whole premise. The headline is the one place §5's landing
      display size is used. The call to action is the waitlist; reading this week's issue
      is the second thing, and it needs no account. -#}
  <section class="front-hero">
    <div class="headline">
      {#- "This week's" on the newest issue only: an issue in the archive is worded
          around its own date (copy audit, 2026-09-28). -#}
      {%- if is_newest %}
      <h1>{{ t("weekly.page.read-this-week-s-news-in-hebrew", "Read this week's news in Hebrew. Every word explained.") }}</h1>
      {%- else %}
      <h1>{{ t("weekly.page.read-the-news-as-it-was-on", "Read the news in Hebrew, as it was on {date}. Every word explained.", date=dated_on) }}</h1>
      {%- endif %}
      <p class="lede">
        {{ t("weekly.page.targum-is-a-reader-for-hebrew-tap", "targum is a reader for Hebrew: tap any word for what it means, say how well you
      know it, and every page after remembers.") }}
      </p>
      {#- The waitlist is for a stranger. Somebody who has chosen targum is not sold
          to again (§6), so for them the Read button leads the hero (copy audit,
          2026-09-28). -#}
      {%- if not signed_in %}
      {% with note = true %}{% include '_join_form.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
      {%- endif %}
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="btn {{ 'cta' if signed_in else 'tonal' }}" href="#embed">{% if is_newest %}{{ t("weekly.page.read-this-week-s-issue", "Read this week's issue") }}{% else %}{{ t("weekly.page.read-this-issue", "Read this issue") }}{% endif %}</a>
      </p>
      {%- if signed_in %}
      {%- with series_id = 'weekly', says = t("subs.series.weekly-says", "New issues show on Your targums and come by mail each Monday.") %}{% include '_series_subscribe.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
      {%- endif %}
      {#- The press line: the outlets this issue cites, as their own wordmarks in their
          own colours — §12 records the departure. Only outlets the foot's sources list
          names; the marks and their licences are `assets/press/`. -#}
      {%- if press %}
      <p class="press">
        <span class="from">{% if is_newest %}{{ t("weekly.page.from-this-week-s-reporting-in", "From this week's reporting in") }}{% else %}{{ t("weekly.page.from-that-week-s-reporting-in", "From that week's reporting in") }}{% endif %}</span>
        {%- for outlet, mark in press %}
        <img src="{{ data_uri(mark) }}" alt="{{ outlet }}">
        {%- endfor %}
      </p>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>
    {#- The week's papers, beside the pitch: photographs of real Israeli front pages,
        because nothing else on the page says "news" before the reader does. Imagery
        (§12): aria-hidden, square corners, and it appears only on a wide window. The
        images and what they are — including that they are not free files — are
        recorded in `assets/press/README.md`. -#}
    <figure class="art papers" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="paper"><img src="{{ data_uri('press/pages/yedioth.jpg') }}" alt=""></div>
        <div class="paper"><img src="{{ data_uri('press/pages/haaretz.jpg') }}" alt=""></div>
        <div class="paper"><img src="{{ data_uri('press/pages/hayom.jpg') }}" alt=""></div>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {#- The page's spine: one small label over each block, in the one register the page
      already has for it (Mondays, Sources). On a phone they read as a table of
      contents while scrolling. -#}
  <h2 class="label">{{ t("weekly.page.this-week", "This issue") }}</h2>
  {#- The dateline: where the weekly is introduced as what it is — the reader,
      demonstrated on the week's news, free, every Monday. The hero above says what
      targum is; this says why an issue is sitting on the page. -#}
  <p class="dateline">
    {{ t("weekly.page.every-monday-targum-publishes-a-free-issue", "Every Monday, targum publishes a free issue of the week's news, written at three levels. Pick one:") }}
  </p>

  {#- The three levels, each a way in. A reader has to choose one before they can
      start, and "Easy / Simplified / Native" alone leaves them guessing which is
      theirs — so each says who it is for. The counts are the ladder in `progress.js`,
      said in words rather than in Hebrew letters.

      Links, and the page's own: for a while the switcher lived only in the framed
      reader's bar, on the argument that two controls doing one thing, with only one of
      them able to know which level is showing, is a way to be wrong on screen. Then the
      bar became one row on a phone and the switch went behind ⋯. The page can know: the
      frame is the same origin, and `weekly.js` reads where it has gone on every load and
      keeps this and the address in step. -#}
  <nav class="ladder" aria-label="{{ t("weekly.page.level-change-it-whenever-a-sentence-is", "Level — change it whenever a sentence is too hard, or too easy") }}">
    {%- for other, other_spec in levels.items() %}
    <a href="/weekly/{{ issue.id }}/{{ other.value }}" data-level="{{ other.value }}"
       data-what="{{ explained[other] }}"{% if other == level %} class="here" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
      <b>{{ level_names[other] }}</b>
      <span class="figure"><span class="for">{{ t("weekly.page.for", "if you know") }} </span>{{ t("weekly.page.figure-words", "{figure} words", figure=other_spec.figure_in(page_language|default("en"))) }}</span>
      <span class="what">{{ explained[other] }}</span>
    </a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </nav>
  {#- On a phone the three are a row of chips with no room for what each one is, so the
      one chosen says it here, once. -#}
  <p class="ladder-what" id="ladder-what">{{ explained[level] }}</p>

  {#- The reader, embedded rather than linked to.
      
      A link away is a decision to make before anything has been shown, and a stranger
      makes it by leaving. Framed, the first thing on the page is the product working:
      the same built file a signed-in reader gets, with its own level switcher, its word
      cards and its vocabulary count. Same origin both ways, and `frame-ancestors 'self'`
      keeps every other site out. -#}
  {#- And given the screen. A reader inside a page that scrolls is the worst of both: the
      page takes the scroll the reader wanted, and the reader is cramped. So the frame
      takes the whole window the moment somebody starts reading in it — a tap or a click
      inside, or on a phone scrolling it to the top — and gives it back from the one row
      above it, or on Escape. `weekly.js`. -#}
  <div class="embed" id="embed">
    <button type="button" class="handle" id="embed-handle" aria-expanded="false">{{ t("weekly.page.full-screen", "Full screen") }}</button>
    <iframe
      src="/weekly/read/{{ folder }}/reader/index.html"
      title="{{ issue.title }} — {{ level_names[level] }} · {{ t("weekly.page.figure-words", "{figure} words", figure=spec.figure_in(page_language|default("en"))) }}"
      loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>

  {#- How it works: three things the reader does, each shown with the reader's own
      piece rather than a drawing of it — the card a tap opens, a line as marking
      leaves it, the level chips. After the reader, because it explains what was just
      touched; before the door, because the door asks you to keep what this describes.
      The figures are illustrations: their controls are not controls, and a screen
      reader gets the sentence, not the picture. -#}
  <section class="how">
    <h2 class="label">{{ t("weekly.page.why-targum", "Why targum") }}</h2>
    <p class="section-title">{{ t("weekly.page.built-for-reading-not-for-drills", "Built for reading, not for drills.") }}</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("weekly.page.every-word-one-tap", "Every word, one tap.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("weekly.page.a-card-says-what-it-means-how", "A card says what it means, how common it is and how it is said; tell it how well you know the word, and it remembers.") }}</span>
        <figure class="how-card" aria-hidden="true">
          <bdi class="lemma" lang="he" dir="rtl">הֶחָלָב</bdi>
          <span class="said">{{ t("weekly.page.said", "said") }} <bdi dir="ltr" lang="he-fonipa">heχaˈlav</bdi></span>
          <span class="form">{{ t("weekly.page.dictionary-form", "dictionary form") }} <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">חָלָב</bdi> <i class="pos">{{ t("weekly.page.moderate", "moderate") }}</i></span>
          <span class="meaning">{{ t("weekly.page.milk", "milk") }}</span>
          <span class="levels"><span class="level">1</span><span class="level">2</span><span class="level">3</span><span class="level known on">{{ t("weekly.page.known", "known") }}</span></span>
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("weekly.page.the-page-learns-what-you-know", "The page learns what you know.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("weekly.page.words-you-know-go-plain-what-is", "Words you know go plain; what is left is what you have to learn. Signed in, that follows you into everything you read next.") }}</span>
        <figure class="how-line marking" aria-hidden="true">
          <p class="src" lang="he" dir="rtl"><span class="w" data-status="9">סליחה</span>, <span class="w" data-status="9">איפה</span> <span class="w">החלב</span>? <span class="w" data-status="1">במקרר</span>, <span class="w" data-status="9">בסוף</span> <span class="w">המעבר</span> <span class="w" data-status="9">הזה</span>.</p>
          <p class="tr" lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}" dir="ltr">{{ t("weekly.page.excuse-me-where-is-the-milk-in", "Excuse me, where is the milk? In the fridge, at the end of this aisle.") }}</p>
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("weekly.page.three-versions-of-the-same-week", "Three versions of the same week.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("weekly.page.easy-simplified-native-too-hard-the-easier", "Easy, Simplified, Native. Too hard? The easier one is a press away, at the same paragraph.") }}</span>
        <figure class="how-levels" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="levels">
            {%- for other, other_spec in levels.items() %}
            <span class="level{% if other == level %} here{% endif %}">{{ level_names[other] }}</span>
            {%- endfor %}
          </span>
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("weekly.page.a-reader-properly", "A reader, properly.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("weekly.page.vowel-points-on-or-off-a-recording", "Vowel points on or off, a recording line by line, pages that turn the Hebrew way, full screen when you want it.") }}</span>
        <figure class="how-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="icons">
            <span class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16"><path d="M4 3.5h8M8 3.5v6"/><path d="M5 12.5h0M8 12.5h0M11 12.5h0" class="dots"/></svg></span>
            <span class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16"><path d="M2.5 3 6.5 5.5 2.5 8Z"/><path d="M9 5.5h4.5M2.5 11h11M2.5 13.5h7" class="faint"/></svg></span>
            <span class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16"><rect x="2" y="3" width="5" height="10" rx="1"/><rect x="9" y="3" width="5" height="10" rx="1"/></svg></span>
            <span class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16"><path d="M2.5 6V2.5H6M10 2.5h3.5V6M13.5 10v3.5H10M6 13.5H2.5V10"/></svg></span>
          </span>
        </figure>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  {#- Where the issue came from: the honesty a stranger deciding whether to trust a news
      digest is looking for.

      There was a sentence above the sources saying the issue had been compiled by a
      model and curated by the targum team before it went out. It was true while a person
      read every issue and pressed publish, and on 2026-09-17 that stopped being how an
      issue goes out (`weekly/entries.py`). The sources stayed and the sentence went:
      saying nothing about how it was made is honest, and saying something that used to
      be true is not. -#}
  <section class="honest">
    <h2 class="label">{{ t("weekly.page.made-honestly", "Made honestly") }}</h2>


  {%- if issue.sources %}
  {#- Where the week came from, and a way into any of it.

      The headline links out because a citation nobody can follow is not a citation, and
      because the outlet who reported it should get the visit. `rel` on every one: these
      addresses arrive from somebody else's feed, so the tab that opens gets no handle
      back on this one, and the link passes on no ranking.

      Import builds the article as the reader's own targum — their account, their spend,
      their shelf, exactly as pasting the link into Add would. Signed out it meets the
      door first, which is the point: somebody who wants to read a whole article is
      further along than somebody who wants to read a digest. -#}
  {#- Folded on a phone, open on a wide window (`weekly.js` closes it under 60rem):
      twelve rows are a long tail under the ask, and a citation behind one tap is still
      a citation. -#}
  <details class="sources" id="sources" open>
    <summary><b class="sub">{{ t("weekly.page.sources", "Sources") }} <span class="count">({{ issue.sources | length }})</span></b></summary>
    <ul>
      {%- for story in issue.sources %}
      <li>
        {%- if story.links %}
        <a class="story" href="{{ story.links[0] }}" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"
           lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ story.headline }}</a>
        {%- else %}
        <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ story.headline }}</bdi>
        {%- endif %}
        <span class="row">
          {%- if story.outlets %}
          <span class="outlets">{{ story.outlets | join(', ') }}</span>
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if story.licence %}
          <span class="licence">{{ story.licence }}</span>
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if story.links %}
          <a class="import" href="/add?source={{ story.links[0]|urlencode }}">{{ t("weekly.page.read-the-whole-thing", "Read the whole thing") }}</a>
          {%- endif %}
        </span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </details>
  {%- endif %}
  </section>


  {%- if archive %}
  <section class="archive">
    <h2>{{ t("weekly.page.earlier", "Earlier issues") }}</h2>
    <ul>
      {%- for other in archive %}
      <li><a href="/weekly/{{ other.id }}/{{ level.value }}">{{ other.dated }}</a></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {%- endif %}

  {#- The door is the waitlist (2026-09-27): a stranger who has read this far has no
      account to sign in to. The landing's own section, from the same file. -#}
  {%- if not signed_in %}
  {% include '_front_join.html.j2' %}
  {%- endif %}
</main>

{% if strings %}<script>window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};</script>
{% endif %}<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('weekly.js') }}</script>

{% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}
</body>
</html>
